w</surname><given-names>EW</given-names></name><name><surname>Thompson</surname><given-names>EA</given-names></name><name><surname>Wijsman</surname><given-names>EM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Bias in multipoint linkage analysis arising from map misspecification</article-title><source>Genet Epidemiol</source><year>2000</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>366</fpage><lpage>380</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11108646</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/1098-2272(200012)19:4&#x0003c;366::AID-GEPI8&#x0003e;3.0.CO;2-F</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Huang</surname><given-names>Q</given-names></name><name><surname>Shete</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Amos</surname><given-names>CI</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ignoring linkage disequilibrium among tightly linked markers induces false-positive evidence of linkage for affected sib pair analysis</article-title><source>Am J Hum Genet</source><year>2004</year><volume>75</volume><fpage>1106</fpage><lpage>1112</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15492927</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1086/426000</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weeks</surname><given-names>DE</given-names></name><name><surname>Lehner</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Squires-Wheeler</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Kaufmann</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Ott</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Measuring the inflation of the lod score due to its maximization over model parameter values in human linkage analysis</article-title><source>Genet Epidemiol</source><year>1990</year><volume>7</volume><fpage>237</fpage><lpage>243</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2227370</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/gepi.1370070402</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hodge</surname><given-names>SE</given-names></name><name><surname>Abreu</surname><given-names>PC</given-names></name><name><surname>Greenberg</surname><given-names>DA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Magnitude of type I error when single-locus linkage analysis is maximized over models: a simulation study</article-title><source>Am J Hum Genet</source><year>1997</year><volume>60</volume><fpage>217</fpage><lpage>227</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8981965</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p><bold>MOD-score results for microsatellite markers. </bold>Genome-wide plot of the GENEHUNTER-MODSCORE results for the microsatellite markers using the sex-specific map. The red graph shows the MOD scores obtained under four-penetrance trait models that take imprinting into account, and the blue graph shows the MOD scores obtained under the nonimprinting constraint.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2156-6-S1-S162-1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p><bold>MOD-score results for Affymetrix SNP markers. </bold>Genome-wide plot of the GENEHUNTER-MODSCORE results for the Affymetrix SNP markers. The red graph shows the MOD scores obtained under four-penetrance trait models which take imprinting into account, and the blue graph shows the MOD scores obtained under the nonimprinting constraint. Here, a sex-averaged map has been used.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2156-6-S1-S162-2"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>MOD scores and estimated trait-model parameters for microsatellites and SNPs<sup>a</sup></p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td>